7592186193767 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193767 #edb/ref 1061 72623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193767 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d13e0838-e726-5176-9bda-6c3c268e6f6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193767 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192842 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193768 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186193769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193768 #edb/ref 1061 72627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193768 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a071a825-5147-5a63-8643-4bf286ad0601" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193768 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192843 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193769 #edb/ref 1061 72631 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193769 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8c216a09-f324-56db-8914-490a9ba85323" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193769 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186192844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193912 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we wish to find the solution to the inequality\n\n$$\n2x + 6 < 12.\n$$\n\nAn inequality like this one can be solved just like an equation, using the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $6$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+6 & <12 \\\\ 2x+6-6 & <12-6 \\\\ 2x+0 & <6 \\\\ 2x & <6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & <6 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & <\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{2}} & <3 \\\\ x & <3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution is $x < 3$ In other words, any value less than $3$ satisfies the inequality.\n\nLet's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193912 #edb/ref 1065 8062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193912 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0d9501dc-3e68-5741-9382-814ea643182b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186193912 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
